    },
    {
      "id": "quJAOKVeEvKd8of98uDTs",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1748.7292717151336,
      "y": 75133.8527630224,
      "width": 1221,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bne",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1717325814,
      "version": 11,
      "versionNonce": 725133622,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1779709733053,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a distributed network of servers strategically located around the globe \nto deliver content to users more efficiently. When you have a server in India and a client is trying to access \nyour content from the USA, a CDN can significantly improve the performance and speed of content delivery. \nHere’s how a CDN works in this scenario.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a distributed network of servers strategically located around the globe \nto deliver content to users more efficiently. When you have a server in India and a client is trying to access \nyour content from the USA, a CDN can significantly improve the performance and speed of content delivery. \nHere’s how a CDN works in this scenario.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "q3zi1YHVot2vZ5jO-XkW9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1745.8073967151336,
      "y": 75309.4230755224,
      "width": 1122,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bnf",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 669649270,
      "version": 1673,
      "versionNonce": 1089088810,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1779709733053,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1 we cache content that is similar for all the users, like HTML, CSS, JS, videos\n2 we will not cache dynamic or user specific data, cdn will not cache that, due to security issue.\n3 if we will change CSS then how CDN will know, lets say we have changed Backgroudn to black but\n    in CDN it is set to blue.\n4 Every time CDN will call our origin server for HTML, so that it has idea of what is current CSS\n    that I need to use. If that version of CSS is present in CDN then it will going to server other\n    wise it will take from origin server cache it ans serve\n5 But the issue is CDN will hit our origin server everytime for HTML, so load will increase, so \n    to avoid that, as our website will change weekly atlease, so we will give TTL of 7 days, after\n    7 days it will fetch HTML from origin server till the time it is going to server old version\n6 If anything urgent we want to change in our website HTML then in that case we can go to CDN provider\n (AWS Front, Akamai, Cloudfare) and tell them fetch HTML from origin server, then it will not serve\n    old HTML, it will fetch recent changes, form or invalidating CDN.\n7 We have videos, for sometiime we want paid user can see the video and free user can't then how our\n    CDN will know, it is a paid user or free user, for that server will send public key or server \n    and using that CDN can check that we need to send videos or not.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1 we cache content that is similar for all the users, like HTML, CSS, JS, videos\n2 we will not cache dynamic or user specific data, cdn will not cache that, due to security issue.\n3 if we will change CSS then how CDN will know, lets say we have changed Backgroudn to black but\n    in CDN it is set to blue.\n4 Every time CDN will call our origin server for HTML, so that it has idea of what is current CSS\n    that I need to use. If that version of CSS is present in CDN then it will going to server other\n    wise it will take from origin server cache it ans serve\n5 But the issue is CDN will hit our origin server everytime for HTML, so load will increase, so \n    to avoid that, as our website will change weekly atlease, so we will give TTL of 7 days, after\n    7 days it will fetch HTML from origin server till the time it is going to server old version\n6 If anything urgent we want to change in our website HTML then in that case we can go to CDN provider\n (AWS Front, Akamai, Cloudfare) and tell them fetch HTML from origin server, then it will not serve\n    old HTML, it will fetch recent changes, form or invalidating CDN.\n7 We have videos, for sometiime we want paid user can see the video and free user can't then how our\n    CDN will know, it is a paid user or free user, for that server will send public key or server \n    and using that CDN can check that we need to send videos or not.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
