      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Because a cache and a database are built for different goals.\n\nAt first it feels logical:\n“If cache is faster, why not store everything in cache and remove database?”\nBut the moment you scale systems, problems start appearing.\n\nFirst Understand the Core Difference\n\n| Database                  | Cache                          |\n| ------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| Source of truth           | Temporary fast storage         |\n| Durable                   | Volatile                       |\n| Optimized for correctness | Optimized for speed            |\n| Stores data permanently   | Stores frequently used data    |\n| Complex queries supported | Simple key-value access mostly |\n| Data safety is priority   | Latency is priority            |\n\nThink like this:\n\nDatabase = Bank locker\nCache = Your wallet\n\nYou keep small frequently used money in wallet because it is fast.\n\nBut you do NOT keep your entire life savings in wallet.\n\nMain Reasons Why Entire Database Cannot Be Cache\n\n1. Cache is Usually In-Memory (RAM)\n\nMost caches like:\n\nRedis\nMemcached\n\nstore data in RAM.\nRAM is:\n\nextremely fast\nbut expensive\nand limited\n\n| Storage | Approx Speed | Cost           |\n| ------- | ------------ | -------------- |\n| RAM     | Nanoseconds  | Very expensive |\n| SSD     | Microseconds | Cheaper        |\n| HDD     | Milliseconds | Very cheap     |\n\n\n2. Cache Data Can Be Lost\n\nCache is not designed for guaranteed durability.\n\nIf server crashes:\n\ncache may disappear\nmemory gets wiped\n\nDatabases ensure:\n\npersistence\ncrash recovery\nWAL logs\ntransactions\nbackups\n\nExample:\n\nBank balance\nOrders\nPayments\n\nYou cannot risk losing them.\n\n3. Cache Does Not Handle Complex Queries Well\n\nDatabases are optimized for:\n\njoins\naggregations\nfiltering\nindexing\nACID transactions\n\nExample SQL:\n\nSELECT department, SUM(salary)\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY department;\n\nA cache usually works like:\ncache.get(\"user:101\")\nSimple key-value lookup.\n\n\n4. Cache Eviction Happens\n\nCaches automatically remove old data.\n\nPolicies:\n\nLRU\nLFU\nTTL expiration\n\nMeaning:\n\ncache may delete entries anytime\n\nDatabase cannot randomly delete customer records.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Because a cache and a database are built for different goals.\n\nAt first it feels logical:\n“If cache is faster, why not store everything in cache and remove database?”\nBut the moment you scale systems, problems start appearing.\n\nFirst Understand the Core Difference\n\n| Database                  | Cache                          |\n| ------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| Source of truth           | Temporary fast storage         |\n| Durable                   | Volatile                       |\n| Optimized for correctness | Optimized for speed            |\n| Stores data permanently   | Stores frequently used data    |\n| Complex queries supported | Simple key-value access mostly |\n| Data safety is priority   | Latency is priority            |\n\nThink like this:\n\nDatabase = Bank locker\nCache = Your wallet\n\nYou keep small frequently used money in wallet because it is fast.\n\nBut you do NOT keep your entire life savings in wallet.\n\nMain Reasons Why Entire Database Cannot Be Cache\n\n1. Cache is Usually In-Memory (RAM)\n\nMost caches like:\n\nRedis\nMemcached\n\nstore data in RAM.\nRAM is:\n\nextremely fast\nbut expensive\nand limited\n\n| Storage | Approx Speed | Cost           |\n| ------- | ------------ | -------------- |\n| RAM     | Nanoseconds  | Very expensive |\n| SSD     | Microseconds | Cheaper        |\n| HDD     | Milliseconds | Very cheap     |\n\n\n2. Cache Data Can Be Lost\n\nCache is not designed for guaranteed durability.\n\nIf server crashes:\n\ncache may disappear\nmemory gets wiped\n\nDatabases ensure:\n\npersistence\ncrash recovery\nWAL logs\ntransactions\nbackups\n\nExample:\n\nBank balance\nOrders\nPayments\n\nYou cannot risk losing them.\n\n3. Cache Does Not Handle Complex Queries Well\n\nDatabases are optimized for:\n\njoins\naggregations\nfiltering\nindexing\nACID transactions\n\nExample SQL:\n\nSELECT department, SUM(salary)\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY department;\n\nA cache usually works like:\ncache.get(\"user:101\")\nSimple key-value lookup.\n\n\n4. Cache Eviction Happens\n\nCaches automatically remove old data.\n\nPolicies:\n\nLRU\nLFU\nTTL expiration\n\nMeaning:\n\ncache may delete entries anytime\n\nDatabase cannot randomly delete customer records.\n\n",
